<TopicPage slug="subjects/math/expert/real-analysis">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Real analysis re-derives the whole of school algebra and calculus with total honesty. The
    school version hides questions a professional must answer: what <em>is</em> a real number?
    What does "limit" mean precisely? When may a sum be rearranged, a function swapped, an
    integral taken term by term? Analysis answers with one weapon: the <strong>ε–δ
    definition</strong> — quantified statements in which every challenge (choose any tolerance ε)
    has a guaranteed reply (here is a δ that meets it). The payoff is certainty: every rule you
    ever used — the quadratic formula, the derivative of x², the fact that 0.999… equals 1 —
    becomes a theorem with a proof, and the places where the rules <em>fail</em> become visible
    in advance instead of being discovered in an exam.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="a sequence converging to e — in analysis, the limit is defined so that 'getting arbitrarily close' means exactly something" widget="plotter" config={eSeqCfg} />

  <p>
    The sequence aₙ = (1 + 1/n)ⁿ climbs toward e ≈ 2.71828 and never arrives. Analysis makes
    "never arrives but converges" precise: for every tolerance ε you demand, there is a point N
    after which all terms are within ε of e. Demand ε = 0.001 and N around 1,400 suffices; demand
    ε = 0.000001 and N ≈ 1,400,000 does. No hand-waving, no "approaches" — an infinite promise
    with a finite certificate for each step.
  </p>

  <h2>From algebra to structure: what the symbols promise</h2>
  <p>
    School algebra (E2.1) treats letters as placeholders and operations as given. Analysis asks
    what the operations are actually guaranteed to do. The field axioms say addition and
    multiplication behave — associatively, distributively, with inverses — and the <em>order
    axioms</em> say the reals are a line, not a cloud. Why does the distinction matter? Because
    on the rationals almost all of it still holds, and yet √2 does not exist there: the equation
    x² = 2 has no rational solution, since any such p/q would require p² and q² to share a
    factor of 2 that their gcd forbids. The failure is not computational; it is structural. The
    missing ingredient, <strong>completeness</strong> — every non-empty set bounded above has a
    least upper bound — is the one axiom that separates the reals from every poorer number
    system, and every limit in this chapter leans on it.
  </p>

  <h2>Manipulation with a licence: when rearranging is legal</h2>
  <p>
    Algebraic manipulation (E2.2) is taught as free: multiply out, factorise, cancel. Analysis
    reveals it as licensed behaviour. Expanding (a + b)² for <em>finite</em> sums is always
    safe; doing the same to an infinite series can be a lie. The geometric series ½ + ¼ + ⅛ + …
    sums to exactly 1 — the walking-to-the-wall paradox resolved — because the partial sums
    ½, ¾, ⅞, … converge to 1, and completeness guarantees the limit exists. But rearrange the
    alternating harmonic series 1 − ½ + ⅓ − ¼ + … (which converges to ln 2 ≈ 0.693) and you can
    make it sum to <em>any</em> target at all: Riemann's rearrangement theorem, one of the most
    shocking results of the nineteenth century. The licence has conditions — absolute
    convergence — and analysis is where you learn to check them before trusting the algebra.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "a limit is a number the sequence reaches eventually." No
    sequence converging to e ever reaches e, and 0.999… does not "get" to 1 — it <em>is</em> 1,
    because the limit of its partial sums 0.9, 0.99, 0.999, … is defined as the unique number
    every tail of the sequence traps within ε. The definition replaces motion with order, which
    is why it works at all.
  </p>

  <h2>Equations: existence, uniqueness, solvability</h2>
  <p>
    Solving equations (E2.5) is usually taught as a bag of techniques. Analysis reorganises the
    bag into three sharper questions. <strong>Existence:</strong> does a solution exist at all?
    The Intermediate Value Theorem answers for continuous functions — x³ − x − 1 has f(1) = −1
    and f(2) = 5, so a root is guaranteed somewhere in between (it sits at ≈ 1.3247, the plastic
    number), no formula required. <strong>Uniqueness:</strong> is there exactly one? If the
    function is strictly increasing, yes — the derivative is an injectivity detector.
    <strong>Solvability in radicals:</strong> polynomials of degree 1 to 4 have formulas; degree
    5 and up provably do not in general (Abel, 1824, aged 22 — the theorem Galois then explained
    with group theory). Three different questions, three different tools, and mistaking one for
    another is where amateur analysis goes wrong.
  </p>

  <h2>Inequalities and the order of the reals</h2>
  <p>
    Inequalities (E2.6) are the daily bread of analysis — far more than equations. The triangle
    inequality |a + b| ≤ |a| + |b| underwrites every convergence proof; the ordering of the
    reals is what makes ε–δ possible (tolerances are inequalities). Two warnings carry into
    expert work. First, multiplying an inequality by a negative number flips it: from x² under
    4 you may not conclude x under 2 — the solution set is −2 under x, x under 2, and the graph
    (a parabola dipping below the x-axis between the roots) shows why instantly. Second,
    inequalities give bounds, not approximations: proving √2 lies between 1.41 and 1.42 is a
    complete answer to "bracket the value", and bounds are what a rigorous ε-proof is made of.
    The arithmetic–geometric mean inequality — (a + b)/2 is at least √(ab), so for 2 and 8 the
    mean 5 exceeds √16 = 4 — is a two-line proof that powers hard theorems far beyond school.
  </p>

  <h2>Sequences: the gateway object</h2>
  <p>
    Sequences (E2.7) look like the easiest topic in the list and are secretly the deepest.
    Arithmetic sequences (add 3 each time) diverge to infinity; geometric ones (multiply by r)
    converge if and only if |r| under 1 — the compound-interest sequence (1 + 1/n)ⁿ converges
    precisely because the growth rate and the interval shrink in balance. Analysis upgrades the
    picture: a sequence converges when its terms eventually huddle inside every ε-band around a
    limit, and a <strong>Cauchy sequence</strong> — one whose terms eventually huddle around
    <em>each other</em> — captures "trying to converge" without naming the destination. On the
    reals, Cauchy is equivalent to convergent; on the rationals it is not (a Cauchy sequence of
    rationals can chase √2 forever and never arrive). Iteration gives the subject teeth: Newton's
    method for √2, starting from a guess x and refining with x ← (x + 2/x)/2, doubles the number
    of correct digits each step — 1, 1.5, 1.41667, 1.41422 — a sequence converging so fast it
    powers every pocket calculator.
  </p>

  <h2>Graphs in practice: models and their error bars</h2>
  <p>
    The practical-graphs topic (E2.9) — conversion charts, distance–time curves, cooling curves —
    becomes in analysis the theory of <em>modelling</em>: a physical process is a function, the
    graph is a claim about it, and analysis prices the claim. Take free fall, s = 4.9t² (metres,
    seconds): the derivative 9.8t is the velocity, the second derivative 9.8 the constant
    acceleration — and the fact that the model predicts a body falling for 2 seconds covers
    4.9 × 4 = 19.6 m is a testable promise, falsified the moment air resistance matters. Good
    practice distinguishes interpolation (reading between measured points, usually safe) from
    extrapolation (reading beyond them, where the model's licence expires). Analysis adds the
    professional reflex: every practical graph is an approximation with error bars, and the
    question "how wrong can this be?" has an ε-shaped answer.
  </p>

  <h2>Graphs of functions: continuity made visible</h2>
  <p>
    Graph-sketching (E2.10) sharpens into the formal definition of <strong>continuity</strong>:
    f is continuous at x when small changes of input make small changes of output — formally,
    for every ε there is a δ. The graph intuition ("drawn without lifting the pen") is the
    shadow of the definition, and analysis shows where the shadow misleads. The Intermediate
    Value Theorem is the workhorse: because polynomials are continuous, bracketing a root
    between two x-values where the sign flips guarantees one in between — which is exactly how
    a calculator's solver, and bisection, actually work. Continuity also gives the Extreme Value
    Theorem on a closed interval: a continuous function attains a maximum and a minimum. That
    theorem is why "closed and bounded" matters, why prices have limits, and why every
    optimisation you will ever run begins by checking the hypotheses.
  </p>

  <h2>Sketching curves: local shape, global behaviour</h2>
  <p>
    Curve sketching (E2.11) is differentiation before the notation arrives: stationary points
    where the slope vanishes, increasing and decreasing intervals, points of inflection where
    the bend changes sign. The analysis version adds rigour to each. For y = x³ − 3x, the
    derivative 3x² − 3 = 0 at x = ±1 gives a local maximum at (−1, 2) and a local minimum at
    (1, −2) — and the second derivative 6x classifies them without a sign chart: negative at −1
    (frown, maximum), positive at 1 (smile, minimum). But school sketching hides a trap analysis
    exposes: a stationary point is not necessarily a maximum or minimum (y = x³ at the origin
    has zero slope and keeps rising), and a function can be differentiable everywhere yet have
    an unbounded slope nearby — x²sin(1/x²) near 0. The graph you draw is a summary, and the
    ε-language is the fine print.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "a continuous function must be smooth, and a function with a
    derivative everywhere must be nearly straight somewhere." The Weierstrass function — a
    fractal built from added-up zigzags — is continuous everywhere and differentiable
    <em>nowhere</em>. Continuity guarantees no tearing, not mildness; smoothness is a strictly
    stronger, strictly separate property.
  </p>

  <h2>Differentiation, from first principles</h2>
  <p>
    The derivative (E2.12) is defined as a limit, and doing one from first principles is the
    rite of passage: for f(x) = x², the difference quotient ((x + h)² − x²)/h = (2xh + h²)/h
    simplifies to 2x + h, and as h tends to 0 the limit is 2x. Notice the division by h is legal
    <em>only while h ≠ 0</em> — the limit is precisely the device that lets the algebra finish
    before the division becomes forbidden. The Mean Value Theorem then converts local knowledge
    into global: between any two points, the instantaneous rate equals the average rate
    <em>somewhere</em>. Its most famous civilian application is the speed camera: if your
    average speed over a 100 km stretch was 110 km/h, you were doing exactly 110 km/h at some
    instant, however erratically you drove — no tracking camera needed, just a theorem.
  </p>

  <h2>Functions as objects: bijections and infinity</h2>
  <p>
    The functions topic (E2.13) — domain, range, composite and inverse functions — is where
    analysis makes its great conceptual jump: a function is an <em>object</em>, not a formula.
    Injective (at most one input per output), surjective (at least one), bijective (exactly
    one) — and a bijection is a perfect matching, which is what makes an inverse function
    possible and what lets us compare infinite sizes. That comparison is Cantor's gift: the
    naturals, integers and even the rationals are all the same infinite size (each can be
    listed, however fiendishly — interleave or zigzag), but the reals cannot be listed: any
    proposed list of decimals, diagonalised digit by digit, yields a missing real. Some
    infinities are larger than others; the diagonal argument is the ten-line proof, and it is
    the moment most mathematicians first feel the subject's power.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "integrating is just antidifferentiating." Riemann
    integration is defined by chopping the area under a graph into rectangles and taking a
    limit — and it fails for functions the Fundamental Theorem's formula never sees. The
    function that is 1 on rationals and 0 on irrationals has <em>no</em> Riemann integral at
    all: every rectangle, however thin, spans both rationals and irrationals, so the upper and
    lower sums never agree. Lebesgue's 1902 reformulation, which sorts points before summing, integrates it easily (value 0). Modern
    probability lives on Lebesgue's version, not Riemann's.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Uniform convergence, the quiet deep end.</strong> If a sequence of continuous
    functions converges, is the limit continuous? Not necessarily: xⁿ on the interval from 0
    to 1 converges pointwise to a function that is 0 up to 1 and then jumps to 1 — each xⁿ is
    continuous, the limit is not. The repair is <strong>uniform convergence</strong>: the whole
    graph of fₙ must be within ε of the limit's graph at once, not point by point. Under
    uniform convergence, limits preserve continuity, integrals pass through the limit, and
    power series can be differentiated term by term — every theorem about "swapping a limit
    with something else" in analysis is a statement that the convergence is uniform enough.
    This is the technical heart of the subject and the reason Fourier series, Taylor series
    and numerical methods have fine print.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Integration's second act.</strong> The Riemann integral classifies its own
    failure: a bounded function is Riemann integrable exactly when its discontinuities form a
    set of "measure zero" (Lebesgue's criterion). The Dirichlet function — 1 on rationals, 0
    elsewhere — is discontinuous <em>everywhere</em>, so it fails; a monotone function with a
    million jumps passes. Lebesgue's measure theory rebuilt integration to handle all of these
    and became the language of probability: a random variable is a measurable function, an
    expectation is a Lebesgue integral. When a data scientist speaks of integrals over
    high-dimensional distributions, the machinery underneath is this 1902 upgrade.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The frontier.</strong> Analysis looks finished and is not. Pointwise convergence
    of Fourier series was settled only in 1966 (Carleson's theorem, for which he won the Abel
    Prize); the behaviour of generic dynamical systems, the regularity of Navier–Stokes
    solutions (a Millennium Prize Problem: nobody knows whether smooth fluid flows can develop
    singularities), and the fine structure of harmonic measure are all live. The ε–δ method
    itself has successors — nonstandard analysis rebuilds infinitesimals rigorously, and
    proof assistants now verify analysis formally, which forces the definitions to be stated
    with machine-checkable precision. What does not change: the discipline of asking, for
    every "obvious" step, <em>under exactly what hypotheses is this true?</em> That habit, not
    the theorems, is the subject.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Abbott, Understanding Analysis; Rudin, Principles of Mathematical Analysis.</p>
</TopicPage>
